From 0000000000000000000000000000000000000000 Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: AltarsCN <337293323+Altars3668@users.noreply.github.com>
Date: Sun, 27 Sep 2026 20:58:40 -0400
Subject: [PATCH] d2d1: Draw gradients with their extend mode, gamma and colour
 spaces.

A gradient stop collection kept its stops and nothing else: every gradient
was clamped and interpolated in sRGB, GetColorInterpolationGamma() and
GetExtendMode() were stubs, ID2D1DeviceContext::CreateGradientStopCollection()
was not implemented and the collections were not ID2D1GradientStopCollection1.
Nor were gradient colours premultiplied, so a gradient to a transparent colour
was drawn as though it were opaque.

What Windows (build 29671) draws was measured with the probes in wine-altars'
tools/d2dgradprobe:

- A position is brought into [0, 1] first -- clamped, wrapped, or mirrored
  back and forth -- and then looked up among the stops, so stops outside
  [0, 1] count only for the colours they make inside it.
- Stops are sorted by position, those at one position keeping their order;
  GetGradientStops() still gives them back as given.
- Straight colours and alpha are interpolated, converted from the space they
  are interpolated in to the one they are drawn in, and premultiplied;
  premultiplied colours are unpremultiplied to be converted.  The first
  collections are sRGB stops interpolated in sRGB, or with a gamma of 1.0 in
  linear light, 8 bits a channel; GetGradientStops1() gives the linear stops.
  The precisions with unsigned normalised values keep stops within [0, 1].
- A linear gradient of no length is half its start and half its end, or its
  mean colour if it repeats; a radial one of no radius is its end, or its
  mean colour; a repeating gradient squeezed into a pixel shows its mean.
- With the origin outside the ellipse, a point on a line from it that does
  not meet the ellipse ahead gets the end colour.
- No stops, a custom colour space, a precision of "unknown" and gamma,
  extend or interpolation modes out of range are refused.

The tests pass on Windows too (in a desktop session, on the GPU), and with
the GL and Vulkan renderers.

Not as Windows: Windows samples a texture made from the stops, so a
repeating gradient blends its two ends within half a texel of where it
repeats, and a clamped one squeezed into a pixel or two is filtered; here
each pixel is worked out exactly, which differs by a few levels there.

